Q: Web view flicker/blinks while scrolling A: <p>Apparently, this is a known and annoying problem related to how Chromium (the engine behind WebView) handles hardware acceleration when rendering modern image formats like WebP, especially when there are DOM overflows.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/4aXdw01L.gif" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/4aXdw01L.gif" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>The flickering often occurs because the browser tries to &quot;re-draw&quot; the layer when the image enters or leaves the viewport. Forcing a 3D layer using CSS usually stabilizes the rendering. If the flickering occurs when the image goes over the edges, make sure the parent container has a clean cut. In your CSS, apply this to the image container or the body.</p>
<p>Therefore, the solution would be to add the following to the HTML code; this would prevent the problem.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;style&gt;
img[src*=&quot;.webp&quot;], .webp-container {
    -webkit-backface-visibility: hidden !important;
    backface-visibility: hidden !important;
    -webkit-transform: translate3d(0,0,0) !important;
    transform: translate3d(0,0,0) !important;
    -webkit-perspective: 1000;
    perspective: 1000;
}


body, html {
    overflow-x: hidden !important;
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color: rgba(0,0,0,0);
}
&lt;/style&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How do I get the `setTimeout` function to work with negative values? A: <p>In JavaScript, setTimeout does not support negative delay values.</p>
<p>If you pass a negative value, it is treated as 0, meaning the function will execute as soon as possible (after the current call stack is cleared).</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>setTimeout(() =&gt; {
  console.log(&quot;Hello&quot;);
}, -1000);
</code></pre>
<p>This will run immediately, just like using 0.</p>
<p>To handle this properly, you should sanitize the delay value:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const delay = Math.max(0, userInputDelay);

setTimeout(() =&gt; {
  console.log(&quot;Hello&quot;);
}, delay);
</code></pre>
<p>Alternatively, if negative values have a specific meaning in your logic, handle them explicitly:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>if (delay &lt; 0) {
  console.log(&quot;Hello&quot;);
} else {
  setTimeout(() =&gt; {
    console.log(&quot;Hello&quot;);
  }, delay);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Summary:<br />
- Negative delay values are treated as 0<br />
- setTimeout cannot delay execution with negative numbers<br />
- Handle negative values manually based on your use case</p>

Q: Is there an easy way to return a string repeated X number of times? A: <p>This should be pretty efficient:</p>
<pre><code>public static string Repeat(this string template, int times) =&gt;
    string.IsNullOrEmpty(template)
        ? template
        : times switch
        {
            &lt;= 0 =&gt; string.Empty,
            1 =&gt; template,
            _ =&gt; string.Create(template.Length * times, template, static (buffer, t) =&gt;
            {
                for (var idx = 0; idx &lt; buffer.Length; idx += t.Length)
                {
                    t.CopyTo(buffer[idx..]);
                }
            })
        };
</code></pre>
